# Notebook

A Blot template, started from the "Translated" template as a base.

This file is also the brief for whoever — or whatever — edits this template next.

## How a Blot template works

Every file here is a **view**. Views are rendered with
[Mustache](https://mustache.github.io/mustache.5.html), whatever their extension:
HTML, CSS, JavaScript, RSS and XML views are all rendered the same way, so a
stylesheet can use `{{text_color}}` just as a page can.

`package.json` is the configuration:

- `locals` — variables available to every view.
- `views` — per-view settings: `url` for routing, and `partials` for small
  inline strings such as the page title.
- `enabled: true` — installs this template when the folder is added to a site.
  Leave it in place.

## Which view renders which page

| View | When it renders |
|---|---|
| `entries.html` | The homepage, and `/page/2`, `/page/3`… |
| `entry.html` | A single post |
| `archives.html` | The archives listing |
| `tagged.html` | `/tagged/some-tag` |
| `search.html` | `/search?q=…` |
| `error.html` | 404s and render failures |
| `feed.rss`, `sitemap.xml`, `robots.txt` | Exactly what they say |

**A view's default URL keeps its file extension.** `archives.html` is served at
`/archives.html`, not `/archives`. That is why `package.json` maps it explicitly:

```json
"views": { "archives.html": { "url": "/archives" } }
```

If you add a view and cannot find it at the URL you expected, this is why.

## Partials

`{{> header}}` includes the view named `header.html`. Partials inherit the
surrounding context, so they can use the same variables as the view including
them.

`{{> /Pages/About.txt}}` — a partial whose name starts with `/` includes the
rendered HTML of that file from the site's folder.

## Rules that are easy to trip over

- **No subdirectories.** Every view lives in this folder's root. Files in
  subdirectories are ignored.
- **No Sass or SCSS.** Plain CSS only; nothing is compiled.
- **Escaping.** `{{value}}` escapes HTML. Use `{{{value}}}` for anything
  containing markup or slashes — `{{{url}}}`, `{{{html}}}`, `{{{body}}}`.
- **No duplicate basenames.** `feed.xml` and `feed.rss` collide, because views
  are also resolved with their extension stripped.
- **2 MB per view.** Larger files are skipped.

## Gotchas found the hard way

These cost real debugging time while building this template - read them
before repeating the mistake.

- **`feed.rss`'s URL is not really configurable.** `blog.feedURL` is
  hardcoded to `/feed.rss` platform-wide
  (`app/models/blog/extend.js`) - every "Subscribe to RSS" link,
  `<link rel="alternate">`, and default menu entry point there
  regardless of what URL the view itself maps to. Remapping the
  `feed.rss` view's `url` in `package.json` (e.g. to preserve a
  Blogger-style `/feeds/posts/default` path during a migration) makes
  the feed exist at that URL while every advertised link still points
  at `/feed.rss` and 404s. If you need a legacy alias, add it as a
  *second* URL pattern for the same view rather than moving the view
  off `/feed.rss`.

- **Tag objects don't have a `url` field.** The `tags` section (on an
  entry, or via `all_tags`) gives you `name`, `tag`, `slug`, and
  `total` - no `url`. Writing `<a href="{{{url}}}">` inside a `tags`
  loop silently falls through to whatever `url` the *surrounding*
  context provides (the post's own URL, if you're inside `entry` /
  `recent_entries`), producing a link that looks right but goes to
  the wrong place. Build the link yourself: `/tagged/{{slug}}`.

- **A shadowed local doesn't fall back to the outer one.** Inside a
  `recent_entries` or `entry` loop, the bare `title` tag is that
  entry's title, not the site's - even though the site's own title is
  available under the same name at the page's top level. Mustache has
  no "go to the parent context" operator, and a dotted path like
  `blog.title` (a pattern copied from `text`/`magazine`'s `feed.rss`)
  does *not* work here either - `blog` isn't exposed as a nested
  object to Mustache in this render path, so it silently resolves to
  nothing (verify with `?json=true` before trusting a dotted path
  like that). There's no reliable way to reach the site title from
  inside an entries loop; design around it instead of fighting it -
  `feed.rss`'s `dc:creator` now omits the element entirely when
  `author` is unset, rather than guessing at a fallback.

- **Fixed-width flex columns don't stack until the media query fires.**
  `.main` + `.sidebar` are sized in `rem` off the configurable
  `--base-font-size` (see below), but the `@media (max-width: 640px)`
  breakpoint that stacks them is a fixed viewport px value. At the
  default font size the two columns already sum to just under the
  640px gap they have to clear, so there's very little headroom - a
  larger configured font size can push their combined width past the
  viewport before the breakpoint kicks in, causing overflow/clipping
  in between. `flex: 0 1 var(--width)` (shrink allowed, don't grow) on
  both columns is the fix that doesn't depend on guessing a breakpoint
  large enough for every configured font size.

- **Alpha-blending a color toward a lighter background has a ceiling:
  the base color's own contrast, at alpha 1.** It can never get darker
  than that no matter how high the alpha goes. `accent_color`'s
  original default (`#6A9718`) only reached ~3.2:1 against
  `background_color` even at full strength - short of WCAG
  AA's 4.5:1 for small text no matter what alpha the sidebar
  links/headings and date/footer metadata used. The default is now
  `#9A3412` (~6.9:1 at full strength), which leaves enough room for
  those tints to sit at 0.9+ alpha and actually clear 4.5:1. If you
  change the default again, check the ceiling first, not just the
  alphas.

## Working on the design

Append `?json=true` to any page on the site to see the exact data the template is
rendered with. That is the fastest way to find out what is available.

Keep the template portable. It will be used on other people's sites, so never
hardcode a hostname, a CDN address, or a site ID. Reference template assets with
`{{#cdn}}/style.css{{/cdn}}`, and folder assets with ordinary relative paths.

## Locals this template uses

See `package.json`. This template only exposes two standalone color
controls — `text_color` (body text, and the header/footer background) and
`background_color` (the reading pane behind the post list and
sidebar) — plus `font` / `title_font`. Every other color in the design
comes from a single `accent_color` local (default a dark orange,
`#9A3412` - picked to leave headroom for WCAG contrast, see below):

- `--page-background` (the outer canvas) and `--link-color` are the accent
  color at full strength, unchanged.
- The whole secondary palette — post-title color, the column edge, sidebar
  heading/link colors, borders, and the sidebar background — is a tint of
  the same accent, each at a different alpha.

`style.css` computes `--accent-rgb` once from `accent_color` and builds
every tint with `rgba(var(--accent-rgb), A)`. No Sass, no `color-mix()` —
just the `rgb` retrieve helper (`{{#rgb}}{{accent_color}}{{/rgb}}`) other
Blot templates (`text`, `magazine`) already use for tinting `text_color`.
Change `accent_color`
alone and the entire palette (canvas, links, titles, borders, sidebar)
reworks itself in one move.

Two hover states don't derive from `accent_color`, using `opacity`
instead: `a:hover` (general body links) and `.site-title a:hover` (the
near-white header title). Alpha-blending an orange accent under either
can't reliably darken/dim them the way it works for accent-on-accent
tints sitting on a lighter background — `opacity` sidesteps the problem
entirely and needs no color math.

Other locals:

- `date_display` defaults to `"dddd, MMMM D, YYYY"` (e.g. "Tuesday, August
  11, 2026").
- `author` — shown as "Posted by …" in the post footer (and as the RSS
  `dc:creator`). Empty hides the byline, so the template still works on
  sites that don't want one.

Layout and type are still those of the Translated base template and have
not yet been redesigned for Notebook.

`sidebar.html`'s "Blog Archive" widget renders the `archives` list (year >
month > entries) as nested `<details>`/`<summary>` trees — collapsible with
no JavaScript. Its "Links" widget iterates `{{#menu}}{{/menu}}` (dashboard Links plus
any Pages on the menu). The widget is omitted when the menu is empty.

## QA checklist

Run through this before considering the template production-ready, and again
after any significant design change.

**Responsive**

- [ ] Test on mobile devices
  - [ ] iPhone portrait
  - [ ] iPhone landscape
  - [ ] iPad / tablet portrait

**Content**

- [ ] Test kitchen sink post styles
- [ ] Test empty and edge states
  - [ ] A blog with zero posts
  - [ ] A tag with only one entry
  - [ ] Search with no results
- [ ] Test pagination (first page, a middle page, the last page)
- [ ] Check the template has good error pages (missing, invalid)
- [ ] Check light and dark mode looks good

**Configuration**

- [ ] Check fonts can be changed
- [ ] Check date format can be changed
- [ ] Check colors can be changed
- [ ] Check publishing options
  - [ ] Image zoom
  - [ ] Image captions
  - [ ] Syntax highlighting
  - [ ] Typeset

**Resilience**

- [ ] Check the site still works with JavaScript disabled (the archive
      widget in particular should degrade to plain `<details>`/`<summary>`)
- [ ] Verify no third party assets loaded

**Performance**

- [ ] Minify and optimize any images used
- [ ] Minify and optimize any third party CSS embedded
- [ ] Minify and optimize any third party JS embedded

**SEO**

- [ ] H1 on index pages should be site title
- [ ] H1 on permalink pages should be post title
- [ ] Open Graph / Twitter card meta tags present and correct
- [ ] Favicon renders
- [ ] Validate `feed.rss` and `sitemap.xml` are well-formed

**Cross-browser**

- [ ] Spot check in Safari, Firefox, and Chrome

**Automated**

- [ ] Validate template using Lighthouse (performance, accessibility, SEO,
      best practices)
